# Git, Documentation, and Audit Discipline

## Git / change control

Before work: inspect `git status`, baseline, and branch.

- Keep changes scoped; inspect final diff
- Avoid unrelated formatting churn
- Descriptive Conventional Commits when committing is authorized
- Never rewrite history, force-push, or push unless the task explicitly authorizes it
- If the worktree has unrelated user changes: **preserve them** — do not discard

## Documentation honesty

Documentation must match repository evidence.

Never claim implemented / production-ready / supported / deployed / secure / audited / compliant without evidence.

Use explicit status labels (`PROPOSED`, `OPEN`, `DECIDED`, `ACCEPTED`, `FROZEN`, `IMPLEMENTED`, `VERIFIED`).

Do not hide unfinished work with ambiguous marketing language.

## Audit tasks

| Task type | May modify |
| --- | --- |
| Implementation | Implementation (if authorized) |
| Specification | Specs / ADRs (if authorized) |
| Audit | **Read-only** unless remediation is explicitly authorized |

Independent audits: `inspect → report → stop`.

An audit must not silently remediate its own findings.
